course: chemistry
lesson: 19
title: Fizzy drinks, Mentos and cola
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Twist the cap of a fizzy drink and it hisses. In the factory, carbon dioxide gas was squeezed into the drink under pressure; the moment you open it, the pressure drops and the gas starts escaping as bubbles.
explain: A gas bubble needs a tiny place to get started: a scratch in a glass, a speck of dust, a raisin's wrinkly skin. That is why bubbles stream up from the same spots, and why raisins in lemonade bob up and down as bubbles stick to them and pop off at the top.
explain: Drop Mentos into a bottle of diet cola (outside, with an adult, standing well back) and a fountain shoots up. It is mostly physics, not a new reaction: the sweets' rough skin is covered in tiny pits where thousands of bubbles form all at once.
explain: Warm drinks hold less gas, so a can left in a hot car can burst. And a can of ordinary cola hides about seven teaspoons of sugar: the acid and fizz cover up the sweetness.
fact: Fizzy drinks have carbon dioxide squeezed into them under pressure; opening the cap lets the pressure out and the gas escapes with a hiss.
fact: Shaking a bottle makes many tiny bubbles that grow quickly when it is opened, so it sprays.
fact: In the Mentos and cola fountain, tiny pits on the rough sweets help bubbles form very fast; it is mainly physics, not a chemical reaction.
fact: Diet cola makes a bigger fountain because its ingredients help bubbles form even faster; the fountain is done outside, with an adult, at a distance.
fact: Bubbles start at tiny places like scratches or specks; some glasses have marks etched in the bottom to make a stream of bubbles on purpose.
fact: An open drink goes flat as the gas slowly escapes; squeezing a half-empty plastic bottle before closing does not keep it fizzier.
fact: Raisins in lemonade rise when bubbles stick to them like floats and sink again when the bubbles pop at the top.
fact: Warm drinks hold less dissolved gas, so pressure in a can rises and cans left in a hot car can burst.
fact: A home fizzy-water machine pushes carbon dioxide from a cylinder into water under high pressure.
fact: Some spring water is naturally fizzy, because the water picked up gas underground.
fact: One can of ordinary cola has about seven teaspoons of sugar; diet drinks use sweeteners with almost no sugar.
fact: A burp after a fizzy drink is the carbon dioxide coming back up; astronauts find burping hard because gas and liquid do not separate without gravity.
fact: The bubbles in sparkling wine are made by yeast inside the bottle; their corks fly out hard and must never be pointed at anyone's face.
exercise: Why does a fizzy drink hiss when you open it? | The pressure is let out and gas escapes | Fresh air rushes into the bottle from outside | The drink starts to boil | The sugar reacts with the air
exercise: Which gas is squeezed into fizzy drinks? | Carbon dioxide | Pure oxygen | Helium | Nitrogen only
exercise: Why does a shaken bottle spray when opened? | Shaking makes many tiny bubbles that grow fast | Shaking pumps lots of extra gas into the bottle | Shaking heats the drink to boiling | Shaking turns the sugar into gas
exercise: Why does the Mentos fountain happen? | Tiny pits on the sweets help bubbles form fast | The mint reacts with the cola to make a brand-new gas | The sweets are full of baking soda | The sweets melt and release hidden gas
exercise: Why does diet cola make a bigger Mentos fountain? | Its ingredients help bubbles form faster | It has much more sugar than ordinary cola has | It is colder than ordinary cola | It contains extra vinegar
exercise: Why do bubbles keep coming from the same spots in a glass? | Gas needs a scratch or speck to start a bubble | The glass is thinner in those places than elsewhere | Those spots are warmer than the rest | Air is leaking in through the glass
exercise: Why does a drink go flat when left open? | The gas slowly escapes | The sugar eats the bubbles | The drink turns into water | The bubbles sink to the bottom
exercise: Does squeezing a half-empty bottle before closing keep the fizz in? | No, the gas fills the space anyway | Yes, it doubles the fizz | Yes, but it only works for cola | No, it just makes the drink taste sweeter
exercise: Why do raisins in lemonade go down again at the top? | Their bubbles pop off at the surface | They get too heavy with soaked-up sugar | The cold at the top pushes them | They swell up with lemonade
exercise: Why should you never leave fizzy cans in a hot car? | Warm drinks hold less gas, so the cans can burst | The heat slowly turns all the sugar into gas | Hot cans melt into the seats | The drink turns sour in the sun
exercise: How does a home fizzy-water machine work? | It pushes carbon dioxide into water under pressure | It mixes baking soda and vinegar into the water | It boils the water until it bubbles | It shakes the water very fast
exercise: How can water come fizzy out of the ground? | It picked up gas underground | It was boiled by volcanoes | It is mixed with sugar by rocks | Fish make the bubbles in it
exercise: About how much sugar is in one can of ordinary cola? | About seven teaspoons | About one teaspoon | About twenty teaspoons | None at all
exercise: Why doesn't cola taste as sweet as its sugar? | The acid and fizz hide some sweetness | The sugar is all hidden away in the bubbles | Cola sugar has no taste | The cold makes sugar disappear
exercise: Why do astronauts have trouble burping? | Without gravity, gas and liquid don't separate | Space food and drink have no gas in them at all | Spacesuits press on their tummies | They are not allowed to drink
exercise: Why must sparkling-wine corks never be pointed at a face? | They fly out hard and can hurt eyes | The cork is poisonous to touch with bare hands | The wine inside is boiling hot | The bubbles burn the skin
test: Ben shook his lemonade for a laugh and then opened it at his sister. What happened and why? | It sprayed, because shaking made many bubbles | Nothing, shaking makes it go flat | It froze, because shaking cools it | It got sweeter, because shaking adds sugar
test: "mentos + coke is a massive chemical reaction innit". What's true? | It's mostly physics: bubbles forming very fast | Yes, the mint and coke make a brand-new gas | Yes, the mint is a strong acid | No, it's a magnet effect
test: Dad squeezes the half-empty bottle before closing it "to keep the fizz". Does it work? | No, the gas still fills the space | Yes, it keeps every bit of the fizz in | Yes, but only overnight | No, it makes the bottle explode open
test: It's 35 °C and a pack of cans sits in the car all afternoon. What could happen? | Some cans could burst | They would go flat but stay closed | They would freeze from the heat | Nothing, cans can't burst
test: In a cartoon, a character drinks cola and floats up like a balloon from the bubbles. What really happens to the gas? | Most of it comes back up as a burp | It fills you up and you float | It turns into oxygen in your blood | It goes straight to your feet
test: Ruby: "this cola isn't even sweet, it can't have much sugar". What's true? | It has about seven teaspoons, hidden by acid and fizz | She's right, cola has barely any sugar in it at all | Cola uses salt instead of sugar | The bubbles are the sugar
test: If you poured lemonade into a perfectly smooth, spotless glass, what might you notice? | Fewer streams of bubbles | Twice as many bubbles | No change at all | The lemonade goes bright green
test: "why do the raisins keep going up and down?? r they alive" | Bubbles lift them and pop off at the top | Yes, raisins are tiny animals that can swim | The lemonade is boiling them | Magnets in the glass pull them
test: Someone says fizzy spring water must have been fizzed in a factory. What's true? | Some springs are fizzy naturally from gas underground | All fizzy water comes from machines in a big factory | Spring water can never hold gas | The fizz comes from fish in the spring
test: Uncle Pete is about to pop a sparkling-wine cork aimed at Grandma. What should someone say? | Point it away, corks can hurt eyes | It's fine, corks are soft | Shake it first so it's gentler | Aim it at the ceiling light instead
test: A friend wants to do the Mentos fountain in the kitchen, alone. What's the safe way? | Outside, with an adult, standing well back | In the bath with the bathroom door shut tight | In the kitchen with the windows open | In the bedroom on a towel
exam: Identify the principal cause of the audible hiss upon the opening of a sealed fizzy drink. | The release of pressure permits dissolved gas to escape | The admission of air causes the liquid to boil | The sugar reacts chemically with the atmosphere | The container contracts as it is opened
exam: It is given that confectionery with a rough surface is introduced into diet cola. Select the statement most accurately characterising the resulting fountain. | It results principally from very rapid bubble formation upon the rough surface | It results principally from a chemical reaction producing an entirely new gas | It results from the melting of the confectionery into vapour | It results from the magnetic attraction of the sweets
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a sealed fizzy drink exposed to great heat may burst. | Warm liquid holds less dissolved gas, and the internal pressure rises | Heat converts the sugar of the drink into an explosive and highly unstable substance | Heat causes the metal to contract and crush the liquid | Heat removes all the gas from the container instantly
exam: Determine which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning a can of ordinary cola. | It contains approximately seven teaspoons of sugar | It contains no sugar whatsoever | It contains approximately one teaspoon of sugar | It contains sweeteners only and no sugar
